WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:06.850
Are opioids a human hormone, a prescription
medicine, or a deadly narcotic? Actually,

00:00:06.850 --> 00:00:12.119
it can be all three. In fact, opioids are
not so foreign: they're found in our bodies.

00:00:12.119 --> 00:00:16.950
Secreted by the pituitary and adrenal glands,
endogenous opioids are neurotransmitters that

00:00:16.950 --> 00:00:24.720
our body synthesizes to regulate our response
to stimuli. Things like emotions, motivation,

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:30.740
and pain, are all regulated by endogenous
opioids. Exogenous opioids, as the prefix

00:00:30.740 --> 00:00:36.050
"exo" suggests, are opioids not made or found
in our body. It can be natural, like morphine,

00:00:36.050 --> 00:00:41.430
semi-synthetic, like heroine, or entirely
synthetic, like naloxone. Both types of opioids

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:45.860
bind to G protein coupled receptors, and the
activation of g proteins is what causes the

00:00:45.860 --> 00:00:50.610
effect of opioids. Here is an example of a
mu-receptor with an agonist bound to the active

00:00:50.610 --> 00:00:57.140
site. Broadly speaking, when opioids bind
and activate GPCRs, the activated GPCRs inhibit

00:00:57.140 --> 00:01:02.420
the transmission of a neuronal signal from
one neuron to another. One way in which opioids

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:09.470
modulate neuronal signaling is through post-synaptic
inhibition. As opioids bind to GPCRs, colored

00:01:09.470 --> 00:01:16.140
in red, potassium channels are opened, and
the positive potassium ions leave the neuron,

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:21.930
thus leaving no signal to be relayed to the
next neuron. Zoomed in, we see the activated

00:01:21.930 --> 00:01:27.560
G protein release its G beta-gamma complex,
which activates the potassium channel and

00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:33.770
causes hyperpolarization. Naloxone is a synthetic
derivative of oxymorphone intended to reverse

00:01:33.770 --> 00:01:40.090
the antagonizing effects of opioids. In action,
while naloxone replaces the opioids bound

00:01:40.090 --> 00:01:46.750
to GPCRs due to its higher affinity, it doesn't
activate the G proteins. Every day, a 130

00:01:46.750 --> 00:01:51.950
plus people die from an opioid overdose. 10.3
million people misused prescriptions just

00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:57.030
in 2018. If there's one thing you can do to
address the epidemic, avoid opioid derived

00:01:57.030 --> 00:02:00.409
prescriptions prescriptions and recognize
that treatment, not punishment, is the answer.

